  Imagine KASUMO has:

User
 ├── Student
 ├── Recruiter
 └── Admin

Suppose every user has:

void showDashboard()

But the dashboard should be different.

Student
Student Dashboard
- Skills
- Evidence
- Opportunities
Recruiter
Recruiter Dashboard
- Opportunities
- Candidates
- Verification
Admin
Admin Dashboard
- Users
- Organizations
- System management

We can define a common method:

class User {

    void showDashboard() {
        System.out.println("User dashboard");
    }
}

Then override it:

class Student extends User {

    @Override
    void showDashboard() {
        System.out.println("Student dashboard");
    }
}
class Recruiter extends User {

    @Override
    void showDashboard() {
        System.out.println("Recruiter dashboard");
    }
}

Now each object behaves according to its actual type.

That's the foundation of polymorphism.

